# Captionline environment variables (frontend)
#
# The React frontend is built with Vite. Only variables prefixed with VITE_ are
# exposed to the browser bundle, so NEVER put a secret in this file. Nothing in
# here is a secret: the three values below are all published on a public page.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development:
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is git-ignored. Only this example file is committed.
#
# Every variable the frontend reads is listed below. VITE_API_URL is read
# directly; VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL and VITE_GOVERNING_LAW are read by
# `src/legal/config.ts`, the single place the legal pages get their values.

# --- Transcription service (Phase 2) ---------------------------------------
# VITE_API_URL  (default: http://localhost:8000)  [not a secret]
# Base URL of the Captionline transcription backend. Baked into the bundle at
# build time, so switching environments needs a rebuild, not a code change.
#
# Local development:
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
#
# Production (Railway): replace with the deployed backend origin, for example
#   VITE_API_URL=https://captionline-transcription.up.railway.app

# --- Legal pages -----------------------------------------------------------
# Both values below are currently UNSET, and the UI degrades safely when they
# are: no placeholder is invented and no unowned address is published.
#
#   VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL unset  -> every page says "Email support is not yet
#                                available. Please check back soon." instead of
#                                showing an address, and the /contact page has
#                                no mailto link.
#   VITE_GOVERNING_LAW unset  -> the Terms state plainly that Captionline has
#                                not published a governing law and forum yet,
#                                rather than naming an invented jurisdiction.
#
# BOTH MUST BE SET BEFORE LAUNCH. There is currently no way for a customer to
# reach support, and the Terms have no governing-law clause, so paid
# subscriptions should not be taken until they are real. VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL must
# be a monitored inbox, and it must match the backend's SUPPORT_EMAIL, which
# GET /api/legal/versions reports as support_email_configured.

# VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL  [not a secret]
# The address the /contact page and the legal documents tell people to write to.
#   VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@your-domain
# VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=

# VITE_GOVERNING_LAW  [not a secret]
# The law and forum the Terms of Service are governed by, as a short sentence,
# e.g. "the laws of England and Wales, and the courts of England and Wales".
#   VITE_GOVERNING_LAW=the laws of England and Wales
# VITE_GOVERNING_LAW=

# --- Later phases (future placeholders, unused today) ----------------------
# Authentication, billing, storage, and rendering services will be configured
# here when those phases are implemented. Do not add them speculatively.

# --- Backend configuration -------------------------------------------------
# The Python service reads its own configuration from the environment. See
# backend/.env.example, which documents every variable it reads - including
# APP_ENV, which production MUST set to `production` so the startup safety
# checks run. Those are server-side variables and must NOT be prefixed with
# VITE_. The four secrets in the system (DATABASE_URL, EMAIL_API_KEY,
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET) belong there and nowhere else.
